WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.324
- Okay, so today,
 we are talking about

00:00:02.324 --> 00:00:04.240
translations.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.057
Now, translations
is moving stuff,

00:00:07.057 --> 00:00:10.175
but we use the word translation,
like, day to day

00:00:10.175 --> 00:00:13.342
to mean, like, translating
between languages.

00:00:13.342 --> 00:00:15.880
But we are talking
about moving things.

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.996
We only want to move
vertex positions,

00:00:18.996 --> 00:00:21.040
not the normals.

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:23.960
So we'll swing back
to that later in the video.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.051
You might wonder
what this object is.

00:00:26.051 --> 00:00:27.577
It's basically three

00:00:27.577 --> 00:00:30.092
cat wands stuck into a block.

00:00:30.092 --> 00:00:32.640
These wands have been
placed in the block

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:35.240
at a very specific orientation.

00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.700
You'll notice that
they're at 90 degrees

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.408
to each other, and they
represent things.

00:00:40.408 --> 00:00:42.330
Paul's not going to be happy
about this, but I think

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.164
this one going up should be Y.

00:00:44.164 --> 00:00:46.140
- The Y axis.
- The Y axis, okay.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:47.647
That's Y.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:49.862
We've got X going across,

00:00:49.862 --> 00:00:52.200
and Z towards the camera,

00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:54.760
and you'll notice these
little things on top.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.040
This is the genius of Cassidy.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:00:59.072
She's hooked this up

00:00:59.072 --> 00:01:01.660
with motion capture markers.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:03.397
We have cameras
all the way around us.

00:01:03.397 --> 00:01:05.551
So if I pull on this wand,

00:01:05.551 --> 00:01:07.552
if I pull it up,

00:01:07.552 --> 00:01:09.536
you'll see, as if by magic,

00:01:09.536 --> 00:01:11.820
the sandwich
behind me is going up.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:13.404
- Oh, that's crazy.
- That is so cool.

00:01:13.404 --> 00:01:14.920
- Yeah.

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:16.500
- And it really shows
what's happening.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.888
So as I pull this wand up,

00:01:18.888 --> 00:01:22.224
I'm adding to the Y's position

00:01:22.224 --> 00:01:24.570
so that the Y is going up.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:27.493
Up, up, up, up.

00:01:27.493 --> 00:01:29.005
We've got the X.

00:01:29.005 --> 00:01:30.978
So as I pull this X wand,

00:01:30.978 --> 00:01:34.260
I'm adding to the X position.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.380
Off it goes.

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:39.472
Yeah, sliding across.

00:01:39.472 --> 00:01:41.241
And the last one
I'm going to try

00:01:41.241 --> 00:01:43.160
is the Z.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.233
So as I pull that out,
I'm adding to

00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:48.260
the Z position coordinate.

00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.345
Uh-oh, it's going to
fall off

00:01:50.345 --> 00:01:52.760
the counter.

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:54.615
Oh, no, I think the customer's

00:01:54.615 --> 00:01:56.115
going to want
their money back.

00:01:56.115 --> 00:01:58.968
So what I love about this
motion capture demo

00:01:58.968 --> 00:02:01.860
is that you can really
see it's, like, adding.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:03.757
Like, that's what
translation is.

00:02:03.757 --> 00:02:06.220
You're either
adding along X...

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:08.600
You could also
subtract along X.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.620
This wand only goes one way.

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:12.662
If I could pull it back
 the other way,

00:02:12.662 --> 00:02:15.060
then the sandwich would
go off to the left.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.772
But really, we're just adding.

00:02:16.772 --> 00:02:18.300
- Yeah, it's all it is.

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:21.140
- Yeah.
- It's just a displacement.

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.633
- A displacement.
Yeah, what does that mean?

00:02:23.633 --> 00:02:25.190
- Well, wherever this thing is,

00:02:25.190 --> 00:02:27.062
I'm going to
move it some amount.

00:02:27.062 --> 00:02:28.578
Like you say, just add.

00:02:28.578 --> 00:02:31.304
Just add some offset or
a displacement of where it was.

00:02:31.304 --> 00:02:33.012
In fact, you could do

00:02:33.012 --> 00:02:35.337
X, Y, and Z
 all at the same time.

00:02:35.337 --> 00:02:37.820
- Oh, that's true.
I didn't try doing that.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:39.440
- So have it go to the side.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.000
Have it go up.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:42.886
How about we do it together?
- Oh, yeah.

00:02:42.886 --> 00:02:44.380
Whoa, look at that.

00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:46.020
Looks like projectile motion.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:48.660
We are launching the sandwich.

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.418
And let's say it's

00:02:50.418 --> 00:02:52.024
moving along Z as well.

00:02:52.024 --> 00:02:53.889
The great thing
 about these cat wands being

00:02:53.889 --> 00:02:56.069
at 90 degrees
to each other is that

00:02:56.069 --> 00:02:58.040
they are independent.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.881
So yeah, as you move
X left and right,

00:03:00.881 --> 00:03:03.673
that doesn't adjust
the Y position.

00:03:03.673 --> 00:03:05.279
So yeah, translations.

00:03:05.279 --> 00:03:07.880
It's all about
adding or subtracting.

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:10.304
But when you look at

00:03:10.304 --> 00:03:12.766
the process for getting
something on the screen,

00:03:12.766 --> 00:03:14.390
it's not usually just add

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.960
or subtract
for translation.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:17.867
It's usually something
called a matrix.

00:03:17.867 --> 00:03:19.460
- Yeah, it's a
translation matrix.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:20.969
- Oh, okay.

00:03:20.969 --> 00:03:23.716
- And translation matrix
is kind of overkill

00:03:23.716 --> 00:03:25.854
because we are working in

00:03:25.854 --> 00:03:28.454
what are referred to as
homogeneous coordinates,

00:03:28.454 --> 00:03:31.272
fancy word for we're doing it
in four dimensions.

00:03:31.272 --> 00:03:33.527
Now, I only need
the four dimensions when

00:03:33.527 --> 00:03:35.340
in perspective mode.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:37.583
So if I'm looking at
a scene in perspective

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.441
where objects up close
are large

00:03:39.441 --> 00:03:41.575
and objects off in
the distance get smaller,

00:03:41.575 --> 00:03:43.228
I'm going to need
to work with

00:03:43.228 --> 00:03:46.232
a four-by-four matrix.

00:03:46.232 --> 00:03:49.382
However, you can play
a mechanical game with the idea

00:03:49.382 --> 00:03:51.230
of matrix multiplication,

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.005
and if I take a matrix,

00:03:53.005 --> 00:03:55.299
set it to its identity,

00:03:55.299 --> 00:03:57.494
so that means one's
going down the diagonal,

00:03:57.494 --> 00:04:00.267
on the fourth column,
the W column,

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:02.274
if I put

00:04:02.274 --> 00:04:05.767
an offset in there for
the X, the Y, and the Z,

00:04:05.767 --> 00:04:07.358
the mechanics of

00:04:07.358 --> 00:04:09.988
multiplying two
four-by-four matrices

00:04:09.988 --> 00:04:12.231
would add those offsets

00:04:12.231 --> 00:04:14.266
to the position
of the vertex.

00:04:14.266 --> 00:04:15.788
- Right, yeah.

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.510
- So, more or less,
a trick instead of

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:19.999
pure mathematics.
It's just a really nice trick.

00:04:19.999 --> 00:04:22.851
And since we're in
four-dimensional space anyway,

00:04:22.851 --> 00:04:25.324
and having to work with
 a four-by-four matrix,

00:04:25.324 --> 00:04:27.976
we can get away
with just making

00:04:27.976 --> 00:04:30.604
a translate matrix,
again, just the identity

00:04:30.604 --> 00:04:33.173
with three offsets,
X, Y, and Z in it.

00:04:33.173 --> 00:04:35.827
- Yeah, and actually, yeah,
as I'm pulling these cat wands,

00:04:35.827 --> 00:04:38.596
like, I'm offsetting
 this sandwich,

00:04:38.596 --> 00:04:41.140
and you're seeing
these three numbers.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:43.432
I think about the distance

00:04:43.432 --> 00:04:45.416
these wands have traveled,

00:04:45.416 --> 00:04:47.000
or extended.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.674
That's that last column.

00:04:48.674 --> 00:04:51.203
- Some people like to complain
 to me about that and say,

00:04:51.203 --> 00:04:52.940
"Well, Scott,
a four-by-four matrix

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.727
is 16 numbers,

00:04:54.727 --> 00:04:56.740
and of those 16 numbers,
you only need three.

00:04:56.740 --> 00:04:58.423
Isn't that a lot of waste?"

00:04:58.423 --> 00:05:00.531
And I'm thinking,

00:05:00.531 --> 00:05:02.735
uh, no, not really,
in the scheme of things

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:05.223
Nowadays, we have a lot of
memory on these machines,

00:05:05.223 --> 00:05:06.989
so it is a little bit wasteful,

00:05:06.989 --> 00:05:09.361
but boy, it saves
a lot of thinking time

00:05:09.361 --> 00:05:11.605
when I just have to
multiply, say,

00:05:11.605 --> 00:05:13.705
a scale by a translate

00:05:13.705 --> 00:05:15.674
by a rotation,

00:05:15.674 --> 00:05:18.094
then do a perspective multiply,

00:05:18.094 --> 00:05:21.681
and I can compress all that
at one big moment

00:05:21.681 --> 00:05:24.880
and then get the projection
onto the computer screen.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:26.640
Hey, that saves me a lot
of time and thinking.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:29.115
It's worth the extra
couple of floats.

00:05:29.115 --> 00:05:32.025
- So when you're doing
not just a translation

00:05:32.025 --> 00:05:34.260
but also a rotation, a scale,

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:36.720
you can combine those
matrices by multiplying.

00:05:36.720 --> 00:05:38.868
- That's the beauty
 of the matrices is

00:05:38.868 --> 00:05:41.193
you can get all of these
different components

00:05:41.193 --> 00:05:44.160
all, if you will, kind of
summed up together.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:46.080
Odd phrase, mathematically.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:47.917
We can sum up all those motions

00:05:47.917 --> 00:05:49.938
by multiplying them
all together.

00:05:49.938 --> 00:05:52.232
(laughing)

00:05:52.232 --> 00:05:54.136
- Yeah, you know what?
I did read about that,

00:05:54.136 --> 00:05:56.676
in the way that
you combine numbers.

00:05:56.676 --> 00:05:59.786
If you add five and three,
you're combining them.

00:05:59.786 --> 00:06:02.341
But when you're dealing
with matrices and quaternions,

00:06:02.341 --> 00:06:04.236
you think of them
as a multiplier.

00:06:04.236 --> 00:06:06.651
Now, the nice thing about
the four-by-four matrices

00:06:06.651 --> 00:06:08.663
is that, I guess inherently,

00:06:08.663 --> 00:06:10.903
it distinguishes between

00:06:10.903 --> 00:06:13.225
positions and normals.

00:06:13.225 --> 00:06:16.200
You'll notice, as I'm
 moving this sandwich

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:18.688
up and down,
that the light isn't

00:06:18.688 --> 00:06:21.024
bouncing off the sandwich
in weird ways.

00:06:21.024 --> 00:06:23.554
Like, you don't want
the normal direction

00:06:23.554 --> 00:06:26.108
to get influenced by

00:06:26.108 --> 00:06:28.575
where you are
as I move around.

00:06:28.575 --> 00:06:30.893
If this is a normal
on the top of my head,

00:06:30.893 --> 00:06:33.196
as I'm moving around,

00:06:33.196 --> 00:06:35.088
the normal is still
pointing up.

00:06:35.088 --> 00:06:37.302
You don't want
that translating as I go.

00:06:37.302 --> 00:06:40.264
You'll get really weird,
like, lighting effects.

00:06:40.264 --> 00:06:43.069
- Well, you know,
 the normal doesn't really

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:44.672
come off the top of your head.

00:06:44.672 --> 00:06:47.408
The normal is positioned
at the origin,

00:06:47.408 --> 00:06:49.201
and is only
indicating a direction,

00:06:49.201 --> 00:06:50.821
so it's just a direction.

00:06:50.821 --> 00:06:53.144
And if I take and
translate that direction,

00:06:53.144 --> 00:06:55.338
it's a whole new direction,
on top of which

00:06:55.338 --> 00:06:56.868
it's no longer normal.

00:06:56.868 --> 00:06:59.380
Normal means it has
a magnitude of one.

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:02.201
And if I stretch it all out,
 it no longer has

00:07:02.201 --> 00:07:03.768
a magnitude of one,

00:07:03.768 --> 00:07:06.007
and it points in
the wrong direction.

00:07:06.007 --> 00:07:08.925
Yeah, so I guess we can
get around that in 4D

00:07:08.925 --> 00:07:11.262
using that W component.

00:07:11.262 --> 00:07:14.082
From what I understand,
if the W component is one,

00:07:14.082 --> 00:07:17.412
then yes, you can translate.

00:07:17.412 --> 00:07:20.190
But if we set the W,
the fourth component, to zero,

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.852
that just kind of
switches it off.

00:07:21.862 --> 00:07:24.331
- It throws everything off.
- Right. Can't translate.

00:07:24.331 --> 00:07:27.209
The annoying thing
 about translations

00:07:27.209 --> 00:07:29.291
and rotations is how different

00:07:29.291 --> 00:07:31.439
they appear.

00:07:31.439 --> 00:07:33.725
Like, as I'm moving these
cat wands around,

00:07:33.725 --> 00:07:35.815
like, the sandwich is going
up, down, left, right,

00:07:35.815 --> 00:07:37.901
and it, it feels very different

00:07:37.901 --> 00:07:40.212
to rotations.

00:07:40.212 --> 00:07:42.542
Wouldn't it be cool
if there was a way to combine

00:07:42.542 --> 00:07:44.912
translations and rotations?

00:07:44.912 --> 00:07:47.647
And what if I said there
is a way to do this?

00:07:47.647 --> 00:07:49.480
- You'd have to explain it,
probably.

00:07:49.480 --> 00:07:51.239
- Okay, yes.
This is the idea behind

00:07:51.239 --> 00:07:53.272
dual quaternions.

00:07:53.272 --> 00:07:56.343
A lot of people freak out
 by the word quaternion itself,

00:07:56.343 --> 00:07:58.434
so when I say dual quaternion,

00:07:58.434 --> 00:07:59.992
major freak-out.

00:07:59.992 --> 00:08:01.545
So why don't we think about...

00:08:01.545 --> 00:08:03.433
Okay, this is
just the origin.

00:08:03.433 --> 00:08:05.392
This is the 3D system,
I could say,

00:08:05.392 --> 00:08:09.334
and you're holding
two planes in your hand.

00:08:09.334 --> 00:08:11.338
Now, if you don't mind
doing this,

00:08:11.338 --> 00:08:14.188
if you could put
a plane right here,

00:08:14.188 --> 00:08:16.812
going along the X axis.

00:08:16.812 --> 00:08:18.712
Now,

00:08:18.712 --> 00:08:21.681
I'm going to try and rotate
this cup using a quaternion,

00:08:21.681 --> 00:08:24.628
and the idea of
quaternions is that it's

00:08:24.628 --> 00:08:27.660
one, two reflections.

00:08:27.660 --> 00:08:29.792
So if you don't mind
putting another plane

00:08:29.792 --> 00:08:31.932
along the Y axis.

00:08:31.932 --> 00:08:34.492
So my cup starts
over here, close to me.

00:08:34.492 --> 00:08:37.572
If I do one reflection
through this plane...

00:08:37.572 --> 00:08:39.763
Imagine this plane
is infinitely big,

00:08:39.763 --> 00:08:42.712
but goes through the origin.

00:08:42.712 --> 00:08:45.151
One reflection,
I end up over here,

00:08:45.151 --> 00:08:46.745
and the second reflection,

00:08:46.745 --> 00:08:48.912
I end up closer to Scott.

00:08:48.912 --> 00:08:50.995
And if, if you think
 about what just happened

00:08:50.995 --> 00:08:54.316
for this cup,
it's actually rotated

00:08:54.316 --> 00:08:57.952
180 degrees.

00:08:57.952 --> 00:09:00.552
One reflection,
two reflection.

00:09:00.552 --> 00:09:02.647
And the idea of a quaternion,

00:09:02.647 --> 00:09:05.552
it's where these
two planes meet.

00:09:05.552 --> 00:09:08.027
These two planes meet
at a line,

00:09:08.027 --> 00:09:10.394
and this line is precisely

00:09:10.394 --> 00:09:13.035
the Y axis, isn't it?

00:09:13.035 --> 00:09:15.732
So that makes you kind
of realize what a rotation is.

00:09:15.732 --> 00:09:18.789
It's a rotation about a line,

00:09:18.789 --> 00:09:20.833
and a quaternion is just

00:09:20.833 --> 00:09:22.664
the lines through
the origin.

00:09:22.664 --> 00:09:24.618
This specific one
Scott set up for me

00:09:24.618 --> 00:09:27.092
is the quaternion J.

00:09:27.092 --> 00:09:29.002
It's a 180 about

00:09:29.002 --> 00:09:30.560
the Y axis.

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:33.851
Now, the beauty of
the dual quaternion

00:09:33.851 --> 00:09:36.089
is that these planes
don't have to

00:09:36.089 --> 00:09:37.750
go through the origin.

00:09:37.750 --> 00:09:39.664
If you don't mind,
if I grab this plane...

00:09:39.664 --> 00:09:41.272
You can keep that one
through the origin, though.

00:09:41.272 --> 00:09:42.872
You keep that one there.

00:09:42.872 --> 00:09:44.423
Now, this plane,

00:09:44.423 --> 00:09:46.341
with dual quaternions,
you can move them.

00:09:46.341 --> 00:09:48.155
What if I say I moved it

00:09:48.155 --> 00:09:50.372
further and further away
from the origin?

00:09:50.372 --> 00:09:52.756
What if I passed it to you,
the dear viewer?

00:09:52.756 --> 00:09:54.256
Here you go.

00:09:54.256 --> 00:09:57.093
You grab the plane for me,
and put it behind you,

00:09:57.093 --> 00:09:58.732
far behind you.

00:09:58.732 --> 00:10:01.412
Actually, could you put
it infinitely far behind you?

00:10:01.412 --> 00:10:03.621
We'll just wait here.

00:10:03.621 --> 00:10:05.122
(laughing)

00:10:05.122 --> 00:10:07.212
- It'll take a while.
- It might take a while.

00:10:07.212 --> 00:10:09.945
Okay, so this plane
that was through the origin

00:10:09.945 --> 00:10:12.346
now is infinitely far away,

00:10:12.346 --> 00:10:15.452
so far in the distance
that it's hard to imagine.

00:10:15.452 --> 00:10:17.450
It's infinitely far away.

00:10:17.450 --> 00:10:19.692
If we intersect this plane...

00:10:19.692 --> 00:10:21.192
This plane is still going
through the origin,

00:10:21.192 --> 00:10:22.701
the one Scott's holding.

00:10:22.701 --> 00:10:24.472
That's along the Z axis.

00:10:24.472 --> 00:10:26.953
If that intersects
 with the plane

00:10:26.953 --> 00:10:28.814
infinitely far away,

00:10:28.814 --> 00:10:30.634
you still end up
with a line, yes.

00:10:30.634 --> 00:10:32.152
- Yes.

00:10:32.152 --> 00:10:34.507
- And this line is infinitely

00:10:34.507 --> 00:10:37.112
far away, behind you.

00:10:37.112 --> 00:10:39.766
Now, if you think about
what a rotation is,

00:10:39.766 --> 00:10:41.927
if we're rotating about a line

00:10:41.927 --> 00:10:43.928
that's infinitely
far away,

00:10:43.928 --> 00:10:47.103
what this cup is going to do

00:10:47.103 --> 00:10:50.016
is translate.

00:10:50.016 --> 00:10:51.666
You can imagine this is

00:10:51.666 --> 00:10:53.528
an immensely large sphere,

00:10:53.528 --> 00:10:56.398
an infinitely
large sphere.

00:10:56.398 --> 00:10:58.951
And if it was that big,

00:10:58.951 --> 00:11:00.833
the very edge of the sphere

00:11:00.833 --> 00:11:03.352
would be a straight line.

00:11:03.352 --> 00:11:05.592
- Of course.

00:11:05.592 --> 00:11:07.094
- Wow.
I know.

00:11:07.094 --> 00:11:08.612
It's still amazing just
to say it out loud, but...

00:11:08.612 --> 00:11:10.547
- A sphere infinitely
far away

00:11:10.547 --> 00:11:12.171
would be a flat surface.

00:11:12.171 --> 00:11:14.132
- So that's what
a dual quaternion is.

00:11:14.132 --> 00:11:16.152
It can actually encode

00:11:16.152 --> 00:11:18.032
not only a regular quaternion,

00:11:18.032 --> 00:11:19.536
a rotation about
the origin,

00:11:19.536 --> 00:11:21.218
but you can rotate about

00:11:21.218 --> 00:11:22.732
a line infinitely far away,

00:11:22.732 --> 00:11:25.784
and that kind of rotation

00:11:25.784 --> 00:11:28.452
is called a translation.

00:11:28.452 --> 00:11:30.299
Yeah, so it kind of
combines them together.

00:11:30.299 --> 00:11:31.816
- Brilliant.

00:11:31.816 --> 00:11:33.675
- If I can summarize,
 what we've been

00:11:33.675 --> 00:11:36.617
talking about today
is translations.

00:11:36.617 --> 00:11:38.398
Translations is all about

00:11:38.398 --> 00:11:40.632
moving things,

00:11:40.632 --> 00:11:43.353
and what you'll see
 in this beautiful demo

00:11:43.353 --> 00:11:45.334
Cassidy has set up
for us is that

00:11:45.334 --> 00:11:47.375
a translation is just

00:11:47.375 --> 00:11:50.032
adding along
a certain axis.

00:11:50.032 --> 00:11:52.222
Adding along Y.

00:11:52.222 --> 00:11:56.192
You can add along X.

00:11:56.192 --> 00:11:59.601
And finally, in this demo,
you can add along Z.

00:11:59.601 --> 00:12:01.392
Falling off the counter there.

00:12:01.392 --> 00:12:02.888
You can also subtract as well,

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:04.681
but typically, you don't just

00:12:04.681 --> 00:12:06.736
add vectors

00:12:06.736 --> 00:12:08.672
to create these translations.

00:12:08.672 --> 00:12:11.341
We do so much in this pipeline

00:12:11.341 --> 00:12:13.428
to get the sandwich
on the screen,

00:12:13.428 --> 00:12:15.251
so many different
transformations,

00:12:15.251 --> 00:12:17.664
that a four-by-four matrix

00:12:17.664 --> 00:12:20.752
is usually how you represent...
- It just seems easier.

00:12:20.752 --> 00:12:23.601
Yes, and because I'm obsessed
with quaternions,

00:12:23.601 --> 00:12:26.952
I've also dug pretty deep
into dual quaternions.

00:12:26.952 --> 00:12:29.581
It's another way to translate,

00:12:29.581 --> 00:12:31.702
and I like that
it kind of combines

00:12:31.702 --> 00:12:34.992
what a rotation
is versus a translation.

00:12:34.992 --> 00:12:38.072
But I got to be honest,
end of the day,

00:12:38.072 --> 00:12:41.192
we do need a matrix to...

00:12:41.192 --> 00:12:43.445
- Yes, unfortunately,
that's what you'd get me to do,

00:12:43.445 --> 00:12:45.811
is to take your dual trans...
 or your dual quaternion

00:12:45.811 --> 00:12:48.032
and convert it to
a set of matrices.

00:12:48.032 --> 00:12:49.652
- Yes, that's true.

00:12:49.652 --> 00:12:51.790
So I guess, again,
 we are translating

00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:54.028
between different
mathematical concepts.

00:12:54.028 --> 00:12:56.033
- Very good.